    <Placemark>
      <name>Mýrdalsjökull</name>
      <description><![CDATA[https://goo.gl/maps/XWL5TvNgY6U5v7EJ8 Mýrdalsjökull is a glacier in the south of the Icelandic highlands. It is the country's fourth largest ice cap, covering nearly 600 square kilometres (232 square miles), and its highest peak is almost 1500 meters tall. It is most well-known for sitting atop the notorious and explosive volcano, Katla. Mýrdalsjökull is visible from Route 1 on the South Coast (and also from F210), sitting to the north of the village of Vík. It is visited on some snowmobiling, ice caving and helicopter tours, and one of its glacial outlets, Sólheimajökull, is the most popular place in the country for ice-climbing and glacier hiking. Katla Volcano is a large volcano in southern Iceland that sit in the middle of the glacier. It is very active; twenty eruptions have been documented between 930 and 1918, at intervals of 13–95 years. It has not erupted violently for 100 years, although there may have been small eruptions that did not break the ice cover.
https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AKcrxGRFwW_8pieM40Fy1jM4jx3ilJYDoFfBjBbob7t8KveoSjWdI5yDP5zs8vcoQBO3FEi-5coFaaAOAo6vtVe58tN-Ea8z3Xl_eERDKNI0I4ovIfOLL48ECBL8Z__4IJx8IbDoH29CUbXdjRfIKqRHUjA2OBWUEkm0uFcmSgiKMigIHUH16sBkUQbe_DjFlgM2MCVYFdl3qlOCj0RHq3zFefsNq5MIQfRWpMV4XGRlCmvrz0MzHdXTzEBE7pM?authuser=0&fife=s16383
